A||||7||speed racer||wheel well cleaning ||||1||
Z||000000||speed racer||03-21-2001||01:31 AM||||How can I clean my wheel wells could I spray some simple green in them all over the suspension and brakes?||||reg||1
Z||000001||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||03-21-2001||01:50 AM||||You can spray simple green on your suspension but try to avoid the brake rotors to be on the safe side.<P>I doubt that the simple green will help much however.  A lot of the sludgey lookin' stuff under there is sludge.  Elbow grease or powerful degreasers will be needed to release that sludge.  I would recommend against using harsh degreasers on painted, plastic, or rubber parts.  Another alternative is to rent a pressure washer.  Just try to be careful not to point the pressure washer into the CV joints, bearings, ball joints, and tie rod ends as you may contaminate them with water.<P>All told, the best way to clean the suspension and underbody is with a mild degreaser like Dawn dish soap, a stiff brush (toothbrushes for the narrow places0, and lots of good old hard work.<P>Have fun, that's what hobbies are for . . .||||reg||1
Z||000002||GarethWright||03-21-2001||01:52 AM||||When I did my wheel wells, I used regular car soap, a stiff brush and a sponge.  It also helped to put the car up on jack stands, so the wheels are lower in the wells, you can get in there a LOT easier like this.  I didn't manage to get All the built up stuff off, but they did look nice clean.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wolfhound||03-21-2001||05:15 AM||||After a good cleaning I lightly spray the inner fenders with 99 cent Walmart flat black spray paint.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Alex4mula||03-21-2001||08:26 AM||||It all depends on how dirty they are. I have done this twice. Jack up the car and remove the wheels to have full access. Use a very good degreaser like SuperClean and spray the area. Nothing will happen to the plastic. Use a pressure washer for best results on removing all dirt. Depending on the sludge you may need to rub it with a brush also. Let it dry and paint your color of choice. I use rubberize undercoating because it stick better. Other paints will start to chip. For a quick black on shows or cruising, spray with WD40 or a good silicon base spray tire dressing (not Almorall or alike).<BR>Clean often to avoid redoing the whole thing.||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroJoe||03-21-2001||10:13 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>For a quick black on shows or cruising, spray with WD40 or a good silicon base spray tire dressing (not Almorall or alike).<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have been using meguiar's endurance tire gel, the stuff really lasts!  You have to wipe it on and stuff, so it isnt as convenient as a spray.  My mudflaps were all faded and crapped out, so I repainted the "Fiero" in them and used the tire gel, and they came out real great...I reccomend trying it.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||WKDFIRO||03-22-2001||04:02 AM||||The same stuff you spray on your tires to make them look nice (I like No Touch)will also make the wheel wells look nice too with little to no effort. Cleans up the bottom strip of Formula noses nicely too! Doesn't hurt the suspension and the brake pads have no problem taking it off of the rotors.<P>Little trick I picked up from the car shows.<P>Also, I found that Simple Green and a good brush really cleans brake calipers nicely! I forgot that they were silver!<P> <p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 03-22-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000007||GT Bastard||03-22-2001||08:55 AM||||I hose mine down with degreaser and then blast them with a heated powerwasher.  ||||reg||1
